## What Happened

Apple removed DSA support from its Security framework (SecTransforms/CommonCrypto). .NET 11 removed the managed fallback that .NET 10 still provided on macOS, so `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on that platform.

## How to Fix

Since you only need to **verify** legacy signatures (not generate new ones), the cleanest cross-platform fix is to use **BouncyCastle**:

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

Then replace the built-in DSA call:

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] message, byte[] signature, DsaPublicKeyParameters pubKey)
{
    var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA-1withDSA"); // or SHA-256withDSA
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, pubKey);
    signer.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

**Alternatives:**

| Option | Trade-off |
|--------|-----------|
| BouncyCastle | Pure managed code, works everywhere |
| Ship OpenSSL on macOS (`[DllImport("libcrypto")]`) | Extra native dependency |
| Migrate signatures to ECDSA/Ed25519 | Best long-term, but requires re-signing |

If this is strictly a verification-only path for old data, BouncyCastle is the least invasive fix.
